  "response": "I used the `crimes scan --format json` output you provided and didn't run the scan again. It has one finding on that asset: **crime_00038**, `raster_should_be_vector` (\"Icon-Sized Raster\"). Severity is low and confidence is 0.85.\n\n## The finding, quoted\n\n- Dimensions evidence: **\"dimensions: 32 × 32 px (≤ 64 threshold both sides)\"**\n- Summary: \"32×32 PNG icon-sized raster. SVG would scale cleanly across DPIs and ship smaller; the current asset locks the image to a single resolution.\"\n- Fix shape: \"ship the SVG source; rasters lose at any scale\"\n- Suggested action (`switch_to_svg_icon`, risk: low): \"Re-author or re-export the icon as an SVG. If the source is a designer's PNG, the original vector likely exists in Figma / Sketch / Illustrator — re-export from there.\"\n\n## Why the new indicator should start as an SVG, not another 32×32 PNG\n\n1. **A 32×32 PNG only has pixels for one size.** It looks sharp only at 32 CSS px on a 1× screen. On 2× and 3× screens the browser stretches it to 64 or 96 device pixels, so the edges look soft. If the indicator is ever shown larger (bigger touch targets, zoom, a different layout), it gets worse. You'd have to ship `@2x` and `@3x` copies to avoid this. One SVG covers every size and pixel density.\n2. **Simple status icons are small as vectors.** A checkmark, dot or warning shape is a few path commands, often a few hundred bytes. An SVG also compresses well with gzip or brotli.\n3. **SVGs are easier to style and theme.** With `fill=\"currentColor\"` (inline or as a component), the indicator can take its colour from CSS. That lets you do success, warning and error states, dark mode and high-contrast mode without a separate PNG for each colour. Every colour of a PNG has to be a separate file.\n4. **It avoids copying a pattern the scanner already flags.** If you copy `check-icon.png`, the new asset will get its own `raster_should_be_vector` finding because it's also ≤ 64 px on both sides. That gives you two flagged assets to migrate later instead of one.\n\n## A caveat from the same scan\n\nThe SVG needs to be real vector geometry. Don't wrap a PNG inside an SVG file. **crime_00035** flags `src/assets/partner-logo.svg` as `svg_with_embedded_raster` (high severity) for exactly this: \"2 embedded base64 rasters inside the SVG (image/jpeg, image/png)\". An SVG like that has the same fixed-resolution problem as a PNG, and the base64 encoding makes it bigger. So don't run the check PNG through a converter to get your starting point. Draw the paths yourself, or export them from the original design file.\n\n## What I'd do\n\n- Write the new indicator as a small hand-written or design-exported SVG on a 24×24 or 32×32 `viewBox`, with `fill=\"currentColor\"`.\n- If you're touching this area anyway, re-export `check-icon.png` as an SVG too, so the status icons match. The scanner rates that fix as quick, low-risk effort.",
